  "summary": "Toledo City Council unanimously authorized the Toledo Police Department to participate in a yearlong study with Flock Safety, Axon, and the National Police Foundation involving 25 fixed ALPR cameras at no cost to the city. Police framed it as an investigative tool, while ACLU of Ohio and a local attorney raised privacy and misuse concerns. Captured data would be stored in a cloud database for 30 days.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The yearlong study will be in partnership with Flock Safety Group, Axon Enterprises, and the National Police Foundation. The city is not committed to continuing the program once the study is completed.",
      "paragraph_idx": 4
    },
    {
      "quote": "With automatic license-plate readers, they can be as invasive, essentially, as law enforcement wants them to be",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "The motion-activated cameras would capture images for storage in a cloud database for 30 days until they are disposed of.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    }
  ],
